@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
title k-Means (Lloyd's Algorithm) — Least-Squares Quantization
' Paper: Least squares quantization in PCM
' URL:   https://doi.org/10.1109/TIT.1982.1056489
' Level: 1

component "Choose k\nand initial centroids" as INIT <<app>>
component "Assign step\neach point to its nearest centroid" as ASSIGN <<app>>
component "Update step\ncentroid becomes the cluster mean" as UPDATE <<async>>
database  "Partition\nk clusters" as PART <<db>>
component "Local optimum\nassignments stop changing" as CONV <<host>>

INIT ARROW_MAIN ASSIGN
ASSIGN ARROW_MAIN UPDATE : partition fixed,\nminimize over centroids
UPDATE ARROW_MAIN ASSIGN : centroids fixed,\nminimize over assignments
UPDATE ARROW_OPTIONAL CONV : no reassignment this pass
CONV ARROW_MAIN PART

note bottom of UPDATE
  Lloyd's algorithm alternates two steps that each
  **monotonically decrease** the within-cluster sum
  of squares, so it always converges — but to a
  **local** optimum that depends on initialization.
  Standard practice is several restarts, or a spread
  seeding rule, and keeping the best objective.
  ----
  **k is not learned.** It is chosen by the analyst;
  the objective decreases monotonically in k, so it
  cannot select k for you.
  ----
  **Two lineages, often conflated.** The cited Lloyd
  paper (published 1982, circulated 1957) derives this
  alternation as //quantizer// design for PCM —
  minimizing signal distortion under the same
  least-squares criterion. The //name// "k-means" is
  MacQueen's (1967), for a related sequential
  procedure. This file documents Lloyd's algorithm,
  which is what implementations actually run.
end note
@enduml
